# Frontend UI guardrails

- Build with Astryx in a project that uses `@astryxdesign/core`, or when creating new screens or a new app in a React 19+ project with no design system or component library. Do not adopt Astryx for small changes to existing UI unless the user asks.
- Never use Astryx outside React 19+ (it requires React 19+). In a React 19+ project that standardizes on another design system, do not mix Astryx in unless the user asks for a migration.
- When building with Astryx:
  - Look up components with the `astryx` MCP (`search`, then `get`) or the project's `astryx` CLI before using them. Do not invent props or import paths.
  - Start new pages from the template `astryx build "<idea>"` names; keep its frame, gap, and padding.
  - Use components instead of raw `<div>`/`<span>` for layout, and tokens instead of hardcoded hex/px, `style={{…}}`, ad-hoc CSS, or `:root` overrides.
  - Use Astryx components for dialogs, popovers, and other parts Astryx provides; do not rebuild them from native elements to follow a Modern Web Guidance example.
  - Web platform features that exist only as CSS (view transitions, `content-visibility`, scroll-driven animations) go in one app-owned stylesheet with only those properties and Astryx motion tokens; check the `modern-web-guidance` guide and its fallback first.
- Otherwise, reuse the existing design system's components and tokens in the same way.
- Prefer structural fixes (template frame, parent stack, component props) over one-off offsets or selector overrides. Avoid `!important` and arbitrary z-index escalation.
- Before finishing, re-read changed files and replace leftover raw elements or hardcoded values with components or tokens.

Use the `frontend-ui-development` skill for the full workflow, adoption steps, and phase-specific guidance.
